INT. FROZEN FOOD WAREHOUSE, WALK-IN FREEZER - NIGHT

Steel racks of frosted boxes. A vapor cloud rolls off every breath. The door is frozen shut.

ROSALIND "ROZ" PETKOVIC (30s, thermal jacket, night inventory clipboard) crouches beside the door, pounding the latch. Behind her, her coworker DENNIS ABERNATHY-VOSS (40s, beard stiff with frost) paces the aisle. Between them, hunched on a pallet of peas, sits a stranger in a soaked tuxedo: LAZARUS BELL (50s, slow-blinking, shivering).

ROZ
It's jammed!

DENNIS
Hit it harder!

ROZ
I am hitting it!

Dennis shoves her aside and throws his shoulder into the door. It doesn't budge. They both slump against it, panting.

Lazarus lifts his head.

LAZARUS
It will not open.

DENNIS
Who the hell are you?

LAZARUS
A man in the wrong place.

ROZ
How did you get in here?

LAZARUS
I walked in. Hours ago. Perhaps days.

DENNIS
Days? We've been here forty minutes.

LAZARUS
You have been here forty minutes. I have been here longer.

He stands. His breath doesn't fog. Roz sees it first and backs away, clipboard hugged to her chest.

ROZ
Dennis.

DENNIS
What?

ROZ
Look at his breath.

Dennis looks. Lazarus smiles, apologetic, and spreads his hands.

LAZARUS
I have found it is easier not to tell people.

DENNIS
Tell people what?

LAZARUS
Only that it does not get warmer. That is all.

Roz grabs Dennis by the sleeve and they both charge the door again, slamming into it together, again and again, until it cracks and bursts open on a blast of loading-dock air.

They tumble out onto asphalt. Roz scrambles to look back.

The freezer door swings gently shut. Through the shrinking gap, Lazarus raises a hand in farewell.
